      "problem": "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}-12x+9=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?",
      "worked_solution": "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=4,$ $b=-12,$ and $c=9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-12)^{2}-4(4)(9) \\\\  & =144-144 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution.",
